WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.127 --> 00:00:02.488
David Skelly,Museum Director&gt;&gt;
I've been coming here since I

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:03.980
was about five years old and I

00:00:04.047 --> 00:00:07.050
walked through the door down
the hall there and seeing an

00:00:07.123 --> 00:00:10.310
animal that size really had a
huge effect on me.

00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:12.090
And I know that's
true for many people.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:21.063
[mid tempo rock music ]♪

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.600
There aren't a lot of
organizations that have the

00:00:23.683 --> 00:00:27.080
talent and capacity to do this
kind of work. These mounts that

00:00:27.163 --> 00:00:31.040
we are taking care of are both
scientifically really valuable

00:00:31.123 --> 00:00:33.170
but also
historically important. And so

00:00:33.253 --> 00:00:36.380
taking them apart has to be
done really carefully by people

00:00:36.463 --> 00:00:39.800
who absolutely know what
to expect. And because RCI has

00:00:39.883 --> 00:00:44.360
worked on so many projects like
this they're just total pros

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:45.070
about it.

00:00:45.255 --> 00:00:52.470
[mid tempo rock music
with lead guitar ]♪

00:00:52.595 --> 00:00:54.097
Matt Fair, Research Casting International&gt;&gt;
Each skeleton

00:00:54.230 --> 00:00:58.340
Comes apart slightly different.
There's a lot of similarities

00:00:58.423 --> 00:01:00.890
between this and other ones
we've done but every time we

00:01:00.973 --> 00:01:02.930
always find something new.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:03.898
[tapping noise]

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:05.023
Something else is
holding it ...

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:06.980
One of the new things is
everything's plastered

00:01:07.063 --> 00:01:10.070
together here. And it doesn't
mean it hasn't happened in the

00:01:10.153 --> 00:01:12.870
past but they're really
plastered together. It makes a

00:01:12.953 --> 00:01:15.260
little bit more difficult
because you have to be careful

00:01:15.343 --> 00:01:17.570
when you separate it off the
steel you try and chip the

00:01:17.653 --> 00:01:22.220
plaster and you have to
do it without breaking bones.

00:01:22.417 --> 00:01:25.378
David Skelly&gt;&gt; It's not just the size
or the number of the fossils it's the

00:01:25.453 --> 00:01:29.060
fact that they also get the
artistry that's involved here

00:01:29.132 --> 00:01:32.593
too, because you're having to
think about how you make

00:01:32.677 --> 00:01:35.763
something that's been dead for
a hundred and fifty million

00:01:35.847 --> 00:01:37.348
years look like it's alive.

00:01:37.557 --> 00:01:41.018
[mid tempo rock music ]♪

00:01:41.102 --> 00:01:43.437
David Skelly&gt;&gt;This hall will look
quite different when it reopens.

00:01:43.533 --> 00:01:46.030
The mounts overall will be
much more lifelike,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.180
they'll represent behavior in a
way that they haven't

00:01:49.390 --> 00:01:53.650
in the past. So I think it'll
be a different kind of

00:01:53.733 --> 00:01:54.970
experience for visitors.

00:01:55.158 --> 00:01:58.703
David Skelly cintinues&gt;&gt;In the big picture
this project is happening because museums

00:01:58.773 --> 00:02:00.670
like the Peabody remain
really important.

00:02:00.753 --> 00:02:04.690
This is where scientists get
together to tell stories to the

00:02:04.773 --> 00:02:07.090
wider world and that's never
been more important than it

00:02:07.173 --> 00:02:10.780
is now. That's what it's about.
And so this is a chance for us

00:02:10.882 --> 00:02:13.885
to update all of
that and get it right.

00:02:14.177 --> 00:02:15.762
[music ends]

